================================================================================
  Part 3: peak fitting
================================================================================

  What you are extracting
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Part 2 handed you 1001 clean spectra per date range. Each has a sequence of
peaks in it, and inside every peak sit three numbers: nu, Gamma, and A.

Two of them are well travelled. nu, the position of the peak, has been measured
for decades and has an established route from measurement into a picture of the
solar interior. A, the height, gets read off along the way.

Gamma is the linewidth, the full width of the peak at half its maximum, and it
is the one still largely left on the table. It is harder to measure, because a
position holds up better under noise than a width does, and it has no
established route inward of the kind nu has. But the width is where the physics
of convection lives. A peak is wide because the mode is damping, so the
linewidth is the damping rate in different clothing, and what damps these modes
is the same turbulence that excited them in the first place. Measure the width
and you are measuring convection.

That is what you are fitting for. A careful Gamma is worth considerably more
than an easy one, and the rest of this page is about what stands in the way of
getting one.

  What stands in the way
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

If the peaks were isolated you could fit them one at a time and be finished by
the afternoon. They are not isolated, for two reasons.

Spatial leakage. Spherical harmonics are orthogonal over a whole sphere, and we
only ever see one hemisphere, apodized to suppress the noisy limb. So when the
Doppler image is projected onto the pattern for degree l, neighbouring degrees
leak in. The spectrum labelled l contains power that belongs to l-2, l-1, l+1,
l+2, and outward, falling off as the separation grows. Those intruders are
spatial sidelobes.

Temporal leakage. Any gap or periodicity in the time series scatters temporal
sidelobes around every peak, displaced by the repetition rate of the gap.

Both get worse as l climbs, and for a second reason too: the linewidth itself
grows with frequency and with degree, so the peaks get fatter exactly where they
are also getting closer together. Past a few hundred in l, peaks from successive
n values overlap so heavily that they stop being peaks at all and become
continuous ridges of power. There is no version of "fit the peak" that survives
there.

  What the fitter does
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

It fits the crowd, not the peak. The model for any one target mode includes the
target itself plus every leak that lands near it, and the leaks do not get free
amplitudes. Theirs are computed in advance.

That precomputation is the leakage matrix. It records, for a given ridge of
radial order n, how much of a mode of degree l' turns up in the spectrum you
labelled l. For m-averaged spectra it is assembled by summing the squares of all
the individual m-to-m leaks, and the sum is truncated in practice, because leaks
die off quickly as the gaps in l and in m widen.

So the fit already knows roughly how tall the contaminating peaks should stand
relative to the target. It only has to solve for the mode you actually want.
That is what makes a high-degree measurement possible rather than merely
hopeful.

It matters more than it sounds. Because the leaks are not symmetric around the
target, the centre of a ridge is not the frequency of the mode. Fitting the
ridge as though it were a single peak does not just add scatter, it puts the
answer in the wrong place.

One more thing the profile has to get right: solar p-mode peaks are not
symmetric either. The excitation sits at a particular depth just below the
surface, and that localization leaves the peak with one shoulder heavier than
the other. Fitting a symmetric Lorentzian to an asymmetric peak biases nu, so
the profile used here carries an asymmetry parameter. That is the asym you keep
seeing in the filenames.

  What comes out
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fits are written to *.errfp files and gzipped, one archive per l range, with 8
l ranges per date range. Splitting by l range is bookkeeping only. It lets the
work run in parallel and keeps any single job from becoming enormous.

